WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:06.270
Here is a puzzle about dynamic arrays.

2
00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:08.630
You keep adding items to the end of a list

3
00:00:08.630 --> 00:00:09.580
one at a time.

4
00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:12.220
Most of the time, an append is cheap

5
00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:14.540
put the new item in the next free slot.

6
00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:15.490
One step.

7
00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:18.230
But sometimes the array is full.

8
00:00:18.230 --> 00:00:21.130
Then the append has to build a bigger array

9
00:00:21.130 --> 00:00:23.530
and copy every item across.

10
00:00:23.530 --> 00:00:25.110
With n items

11
00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:28.280
that single append costs about n steps.

12
00:00:28.630 --> 00:00:32.440
So the worst case for one append is order n.

13
00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:33.280
And yet

14
00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:35.720
everyone says that append is order one.

15
00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:37.510
How can both be true?

16
00:00:38.090 --> 00:00:40.680
The answer is amortized analysis.

17
00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:43.856
It asks not what one operation costs

18
00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:45.618
but what a long sequence of

19
00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:47.810
operations costs in total.

20
00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:49.730
In this video we'll count the

21
00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:51.853
total cost of many appends

22
00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:53.910
see how cheap appends can pay for

23
00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:55.393
the slow ones in advance

24
00:00:55.450 --> 00:00:57.700
and find out why doubling the capacity

25
00:00:57.750 --> 00:00:59.600
is the right growth rule.

26
00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:02.310
Here is the array we'll analyze.

27
00:01:02.310 --> 00:01:04.570
It starts with room for one item.

28
00:01:04.570 --> 00:01:05.949
When it is full

29
00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:07.615
it allocates a new array with

30
00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:09.030
twice the capacity

31
00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:11.270
copies the old items across

32
00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:13.060
and then stores the new one.

33
00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:15.740
Let's count the cost of each append

34
00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:17.200
one step for the store

35
00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:20.130
plus one step for each item copied.

36
00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:22.990
The first append costs one.

37
00:01:22.990 --> 00:01:25.870
The second finds the array full

38
00:01:25.870 --> 00:01:29.970
copies one item and stores one: cost two.

39
00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:33.770
The third copies two: cost three.

40
00:01:33.770 --> 00:01:36.710
The fourth fits: cost one.

41
00:01:36.710 --> 00:01:41.480
Then the fifth copies four items: cost five.

42
00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:43.690
Look at the pattern.

43
00:01:43.690 --> 00:01:45.490
Expensive appends are rare

44
00:01:45.490 --> 00:01:48.330
and they get further apart: after the fifth

45
00:01:48.330 --> 00:01:50.510
the next expensive one is the ninth

46
00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:52.160
then the seventeenth.

47
00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:55.490
Let's add up the cost of n appends.

48
00:01:55.490 --> 00:01:57.740
This is called the aggregate method.

49
00:01:58.090 --> 00:02:00.240
Every append stores its item.

50
00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:02.330
That's n steps in total.

51
00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:06.110
Copying happens only when the array is full

52
00:02:06.110 --> 00:02:10.490
and it is full only when it holds one, two

53
00:02:10.490 --> 00:02:13.170
four, eight items, and so on

54
00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:14.550
doubling each time.

55
00:02:14.550 --> 00:02:18.560
All of those sizes are less than n.

56
00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:21.407
That is a geometric series

57
00:02:21.490 --> 00:02:23.708
and it adds up to less than

58
00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:25.230
twice its biggest term.

59
00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:28.760
So the copies add up to less than two n.

60
00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:32.930
The total is less than n plus two n

61
00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:34.790
which is three n.

62
00:02:34.790 --> 00:02:37.030
Divide by the n appends

63
00:02:37.030 --> 00:02:41.070
under three steps per append, on average.

64
00:02:41.070 --> 00:02:42.920
That is order one.

65
00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:45.310
There is another way to see this

66
00:02:45.310 --> 00:02:47.408
and it works like a bank account.

67
00:02:47.470 --> 00:02:49.669
Charge every append a fixed

68
00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:51.048
price of three coins

69
00:02:51.110 --> 00:02:52.792
even though some appends cost

70
00:02:52.850 --> 00:02:55.220
more and some cost less.

71
00:02:55.570 --> 00:02:58.160
One coin pays for storing the item.

72
00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:00.800
The other two coins are saved

73
00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:02.290
sitting on the new item.

74
00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:04.750
Now the array fills up.

75
00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:07.330
Suppose its capacity is c.

76
00:03:07.330 --> 00:03:09.370
Since the last resize

77
00:03:09.370 --> 00:03:12.130
c over two new items were added

78
00:03:12.130 --> 00:03:14.910
and each carries two saved coins.

79
00:03:14.910 --> 00:03:17.520
That's c coins in total.

80
00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:22.230
Copying c items costs exactly c steps.

81
00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:25.230
So the saved coins pay for the whole copy

82
00:03:25.230 --> 00:03:27.960
and the bank balance never goes negative.

83
00:03:28.310 --> 00:03:30.560
A fixed price of three coins

84
00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:32.053
per append is enough.

85
00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:34.040
So the amortized cost of

86
00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:36.070
an append is order one.

87
00:03:36.650 --> 00:03:38.800
Now compare with a different rule

88
00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:40.600
when the array is full

89
00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:43.410
make it just one slot bigger.

90
00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:46.002
Then every append after the first

91
00:03:46.070 --> 00:03:49.219
finds the array full, and copies everything.

92
00:03:49.290 --> 00:03:51.114
The copies add up to one

93
00:03:51.190 --> 00:03:53.030
plus two plus three

94
00:03:53.030 --> 00:03:55.120
all the way to n minus one.

95
00:03:55.470 --> 00:03:58.751
That is n times n minus one over two

96
00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:00.956
the same sum we met when counting

97
00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:02.780
pairs in the first video.

98
00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:05.380
About n squared over two in total

99
00:04:05.380 --> 00:04:08.710
so about n over two per append.

100
00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:10.690
For a thousand items

101
00:04:10.690 --> 00:04:13.730
that's nearly five hundred thousand copies.

102
00:04:13.730 --> 00:04:16.690
The cost per append is not constant at all.

103
00:04:16.690 --> 00:04:17.980
It grows with n.

104
00:04:18.330 --> 00:04:20.514
What matters is that the capacity

105
00:04:20.580 --> 00:04:22.547
grows by a constant factor.

106
00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:25.210
Then every resize buys a lot of cheap

107
00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:27.060
appends before the next one.

108
00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:29.850
Adding a constant amount does not.

109
00:04:30.430 --> 00:04:32.236
So what does amortized order

110
00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:34.030
one actually mean?

111
00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:37.771
It means: for any sequence of n operations

112
00:04:37.850 --> 00:04:39.408
the total cost is at most

113
00:04:39.470 --> 00:04:41.130
n times a constant.

114
00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:43.727
Some single operations may be expensive

115
00:04:43.790 --> 00:04:45.774
but the cost averaged over

116
00:04:45.850 --> 00:04:47.420
the sequence is small.

117
00:04:47.770 --> 00:04:49.376
This is a guarantee about

118
00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:50.940
the worst sequence.

119
00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:52.460
It is not about luck

120
00:04:52.460 --> 00:04:56.220
and it is not an average over random inputs.

121
00:04:56.220 --> 00:04:59.730
No input can make the total cost bigger.

122
00:05:00.330 --> 00:05:01.640
Be careful, though.

123
00:05:01.640 --> 00:05:05.320
It says nothing about any single operation.

124
00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:07.897
One append can still take order n steps

125
00:05:07.960 --> 00:05:11.033
so a program that needs every single operation

126
00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:13.650
to be fast cannot rely on it.

127
00:05:14.230 --> 00:05:15.580
To sum up.

128
00:05:15.580 --> 00:05:19.200
One slow append does not make append slow

129
00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:21.540
because slow appends are rare

130
00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:23.430
and the cheap ones pay for them.

131
00:05:23.780 --> 00:05:26.510
The aggregate method adds up all the costs.

132
00:05:26.510 --> 00:05:29.582
The accounting method charges a fixed price.

133
00:05:29.650 --> 00:05:32.203
Both give under three steps per append

134
00:05:32.270 --> 00:05:34.060
when the capacity doubles.

135
00:05:34.410 --> 00:05:35.881
And growing by a constant

136
00:05:35.940 --> 00:05:37.520
factor is essential.

137
00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:41.170
Growing by one gives order n per append.

138
00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:42.682
In the next part

139
00:05:42.750 --> 00:05:44.312
we build this array in code

140
00:05:44.370 --> 00:05:46.080
and count every copy.

141
00:05:46.707 --> 00:05:49.177
Now let's build this array in Python

142
00:05:49.177 --> 00:05:51.467
and count every copy it makes.

143
00:05:52.047 --> 00:05:53.917
Here is append.

144
00:05:53.917 --> 00:05:56.417
If the size equals the capacity

145
00:05:56.417 --> 00:05:59.035
the array is full, so we first resize it

146
00:05:59.097 --> 00:06:00.595
to whatever capacity the

147
00:06:00.657 --> 00:06:02.127
growth rule gives us.

148
00:06:02.197 --> 00:06:04.104
Then we store the item and

149
00:06:04.177 --> 00:06:05.687
add one to the size.

150
00:06:06.037 --> 00:06:08.392
The growth rule is passed in as a function

151
00:06:08.447 --> 00:06:10.303
so we can try different rules

152
00:06:10.367 --> 00:06:11.637
with the same class.

153
00:06:12.217 --> 00:06:14.489
Resize makes a brand new list

154
00:06:14.567 --> 00:06:17.724
of the new capacity, filled with None.

155
00:06:17.807 --> 00:06:19.938
We use a plain list only

156
00:06:20.027 --> 00:06:21.447
as fixed-size storage

157
00:06:21.447 --> 00:06:23.233
and never let it grow by itself

158
00:06:23.287 --> 00:06:25.422
because Python's own list already

159
00:06:25.487 --> 00:06:27.897
uses exactly this trick.

160
00:06:28.247 --> 00:06:31.057
Then it copies the items one by one

161
00:06:31.057 --> 00:06:34.569
adding one to a counter for every copy.

162
00:06:34.657 --> 00:06:36.114
Finally the new storage

163
00:06:36.177 --> 00:06:37.727
replaces the old one.

164
00:06:38.307 --> 00:06:40.397
We try two growth rules.

165
00:06:40.397 --> 00:06:43.507
Double the capacity, or add one.

166
00:06:43.857 --> 00:06:45.699
Total copies takes a growth

167
00:06:45.767 --> 00:06:47.607
rule and a number n.

168
00:06:47.607 --> 00:06:49.687
It appends n items

169
00:06:49.687 --> 00:06:52.257
and returns how many copies were made.

170
00:06:52.837 --> 00:06:54.467
Here are the real numbers

171
00:06:54.467 --> 00:06:56.147
for four sizes of n.

172
00:06:56.147 --> 00:06:58.727
The columns show the total copies

173
00:06:58.727 --> 00:07:00.887
and the copies per append

174
00:07:00.887 --> 00:07:02.437
for each growth rule.

175
00:07:02.787 --> 00:07:03.916
With doubling

176
00:07:03.997 --> 00:07:05.746
a hundred appends made one hundred

177
00:07:05.797 --> 00:07:07.197
twenty-seven copies.

178
00:07:07.197 --> 00:07:09.037
For a thousand appends

179
00:07:09.037 --> 00:07:10.497
one thousand twenty-three

180
00:07:10.497 --> 00:07:13.147
about one copy per append.

181
00:07:13.497 --> 00:07:15.667
The worst row is a thousand

182
00:07:15.747 --> 00:07:17.007
and twenty-five items

183
00:07:17.007 --> 00:07:19.167
two thousand forty-seven copies

184
00:07:19.167 --> 00:07:21.447
almost exactly two per append.

185
00:07:21.447 --> 00:07:23.347
It never goes above two

186
00:07:23.347 --> 00:07:25.617
just as the aggregate argument promised.

187
00:07:25.967 --> 00:07:27.458
Now adding one.

188
00:07:27.557 --> 00:07:30.028
A thousand appends made four hundred

189
00:07:30.097 --> 00:07:32.877
ninety-nine thousand, five hundred copies

190
00:07:32.877 --> 00:07:34.757
about five hundred per append.

191
00:07:34.757 --> 00:07:36.297
At four thousand

192
00:07:36.297 --> 00:07:38.437
almost eight million copies

193
00:07:38.437 --> 00:07:40.477
nearly two thousand per append.

194
00:07:40.477 --> 00:07:43.727
That is n over two, and it keeps growing.

195
00:07:44.307 --> 00:07:45.737
So in Python

196
00:07:45.737 --> 00:07:48.117
the numbers agree with the analysis.

197
00:07:48.117 --> 00:07:51.057
Doubling stays under two copies per append

198
00:07:51.057 --> 00:07:54.427
and growing by one costs about n over two.

199
00:07:54.777 --> 00:07:56.903
The tests in the repository check

200
00:07:56.967 --> 00:07:58.727
exactly these bounds

201
00:07:58.727 --> 00:08:01.022
at most two n copies for doubling

202
00:08:01.087 --> 00:08:03.761
and exactly n times n minus one

203
00:08:03.847 --> 00:08:06.057
over two for adding one.

204
00:08:06.407 --> 00:08:07.637
In the next video

205
00:08:07.637 --> 00:08:09.877
we learn how to prove claims like these

206
00:08:09.877 --> 00:08:12.527
using induction and loop invariants.

